---
// LaneTutorial.astro — the "?" bubble's dialog: ≤4 slides, one lane.
//
// A NATIVE <dialog>, and that is the whole accessibility argument. `showModal()`
// gives focus trapping, Escape-to-close, inertness of the page behind, and the
// correct role — four things a div-with-a-backdrop has to reimplement badly.
// Nothing here is a framework component; the deck is markup and the only script
// is open, close, and step.
//
// THE SLIDES ARE ALL IN THE MARKUP, one shown at a time. A dialog that fetched
// or built its slides would be a second way for this page to be wrong. A reader
// with the script off never opens THIS dialog; they get the same slides as a
// plain list from LaneHead.astro (its "?" is a link to it), so the deck is
// reachable either way and never shown twice.
//
// The verbs on the slides are the office's own, cited in src/lib/civic-tutorial.mjs.
import { paint, SPRITE_W, SPRITE_H } from "@/lib/civic-art.mjs";
import { CALL_LABEL } from "@/lib/civic-tutorial.mjs";

const { lane, slides = [] } = Astro.props;
---
<dialog class="c-tut" id={`tut-${lane.key}`} aria-labelledby={`tut-${lane.key}-h`}>
  <form method="dialog" class="c-tut-x"><button aria-label="Close">×</button></form>

  <p class="c-tut-kicker" id={`tut-${lane.key}-h`}>How {lane.name} works</p>

  <ol class="c-tut-deck" data-deck={lane.key}>
    {slides.map((s, i) => (
      <li class="c-tut-slide" data-slide={i} hidden={i > 0}>
        {/* THE LANE'S OWN BUILDING, painted from the same sprite map the
            quarter above is painted from — so the reader is looking at the
            thing they clicked. No external asset, no second drawing. */}
        <span class="c-tut-art" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${SPRITE_W} ${SPRITE_H}`} shape-rendering="crispEdges" focusable="false">
            {paint(lane.key).map((r) => <rect x={r.x} y={r.y} width={r.w} height="1" fill={r.fill} />)}
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div class="c-tut-say">
          <p class="c-tut-step">
            <span class="c-tut-n">{i + 1}</span>{s.step}
            {s.step_note && <span class="c-tut-note">{s.step_note}</span>}
          </p>
          <p class="c-tut-line">{s.say}</p>
          {/* A CALL, WHERE THERE IS ONE. A step with no door on the train
              carries no code block at all rather than a plausible-looking call
              the office would refuse — see civic-tutorial.mjs's bounty deck. */}
          {s.call && <p class="c-tut-call-label">{CALL_LABEL}</p>}
          {s.call && <code class="c-tut-call">{s.call}</code>}
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <nav class="c-tut-nav">
    <button type="button" class="c-tut-prev" disabled>← back</button>
    <span class="c-tut-dots">
      {slides.map((_, i) => <i class:list={["c-tut-dot", i === 0 && "is-on"]} aria-hidden="true"></i>)}
    </span>
    <button type="button" class="c-tut-next">next →</button>
  </nav>
</dialog>

<style>
  /* ── NOTHING SCROLLS SIDEWAYS, ANYWHERE ───────────────────────────────────
     THE FOUNDER, 2026-09-01, and the fault he read was IN HERE.

     REPRODUCED BEFORE IT WAS TOUCHED, at 390px and 768px, on the base build
     AND on the branch (identical numbers, so it is the base's fault and not
     this lane's): the deck and the showing slide measure 518px inside a 297px
     content box on the Think Tank's deck and 453px on the Bounty Board's, and
     `.c-tut-say`'s children render out to x=565 in a 390px window. The reader
     sees the sentence run off the right of the screen.

     THE CAUSE IS THE `<code>`, NOT THE WIDTH, and the asymmetry is what proves
     it rather than a plausible story: the QUEST deck at the same width has no
     rail at all. The three decks differ only in how long their longest `call`
     is — ideas 65 chars, bounties 53, quests 45 — and the rail's size tracks
     that ordering exactly. A width fault would hit all three equally.

     The mechanism: `.c-tut-call` is `white-space: pre`, so it is one
     unbreakable box as wide as its longest line. It sits in the `1fr` track of
     the slide's grid, and a `1fr` track has an implicit `min-width: auto` —
     the track cannot shrink below its content's min-content, so instead of the
     code scrolling inside its own `overflow-x: auto`, the TRACK grows and
     takes the slide, the deck and the reader's eye with it. `overflow-x: auto`
     on the code never engages, which is why this was invisible to reading: the
     rule that would have contained it is present and correct and unreachable.

     THREE CHANGES, and each one closes a different half of it:
       · the call WRAPS (`pre-wrap` + `overflow-wrap: anywhere`) — a door's
         grammar broken across two lines is still the door's grammar, and it is
         better than a grammar the reader cannot see the end of;
       · the track is `minmax(0, 1fr)`, so the next long token — a slug, a URL,
         a mark id somebody adds in a year — cannot do this again;
       · the dialog is sized to the VIEWPORT (`min(92vw, 40rem)`) with an
         explicit `max-height: 90vh` and its own vertical scroll, so a tall
         deck scrolls DOWN inside the box rather than growing past the screen.

     The UA's default `max-height: calc(100% - 42px)` was doing the vertical
     job by accident; an explicit value is the difference between a behaviour
     and a decision. */
  .c-tut {
    width: min(92vw, 40rem);
    max-width: 40rem;
    max-height: 90vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.5rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--lane-edge-open, rgba(232, 196, 139, .4));
    border-radius: 14px;
    background: linear-gradient(var(--pm-harbor, #0d1426), var(--pm-night, #070b15));
    color: rgba(244, 233, 215, .9);
  }
  .c-tut::backdrop { background: rgba(4, 7, 14, .72); }

  .c-tut-x { position: absolute; top: .5rem; right: .6rem; margin: 0; }
  .c-tut-x button {
    background: none; border: 0; cursor: pointer; font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1;
    color: rgba(205, 194, 171, .6); padding: .2rem .4rem;
  }
  .c-tut-x button:hover { color: var(--pm-gold-bright); }
  .c-tut-x button:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--pm-gold); outline-offset: 2px; }

  .c-tut-kicker {
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .16em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(232, 196, 139, .8);
  }

  .c-tut-deck { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; min-height: 11rem; }
  /* `minmax(0, 1fr)`, NOT `1fr` — see § NOTHING SCROLLS SIDEWAYS above. And the
     SPRITE SCALES WITH THE DIALOG rather than holding a fixed 4.5rem column: at
     390px that column was 12% of the whole box and the sentence beside it had
     what was left. `clamp` keeps it a building at 1280 and a thumbnail at 390,
     with no second breakpoint to keep in step with the first. */
  .c-tut-slide {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: clamp(2.6rem, 11%, 4.5rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.1rem; align-items: start;
  }
  .c-tut-slide[hidden] { display: none; }
  .c-tut-art svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 1; image-rendering: pixelated; }

  .c-tut-step {
    margin: 0 0 .5rem; display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .55em; flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-family: var(--pm-serif); font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--pm-gold-bright);
  }
  .c-tut-n {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .66rem; letter-spacing: .1em;
    color: rgba(205, 194, 171, .5);
  }
  .c-tut-note {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .58rem; letter-spacing: .12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, .55);
    border: 1px dashed rgba(205, 194, 171, .3); border-radius: 999px; padding: .15em .55em;
  }
  .c-tut-line { margin: 0; font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  /* IT WRAPS. `overflow-x: auto` is gone with `white-space: pre` — a scroller
     around content that now wraps is a scrollbar that can never appear, which
     is the same species of dead rule this page has already shipped twice. */
  .c-tut-call-label {
    margin: .7rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: var(--civic-label, .7rem); letter-spacing: .12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(205, 194, 171, .6);
  }
  .c-tut-call {
    display: block; margin: .3rem 0 0; padding: .55rem .7rem;
    background: rgba(7, 11, 21, .6); border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .18);
    border-radius: 7px; font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .72rem;
    line-height: 1.6; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    color: rgba(232, 196, 139, .9);
  }

  .c-tut-nav {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.2rem; padding-top: .9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .14);
  }
  .c-tut-nav button {
    font-family: var(--pm-mono); font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .08em;
    padding: .45em 1em; border-radius: 999px; cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid rgba(232, 196, 139, .32); background: transparent;
    color: var(--pm-gold-bright);
  }
  .c-tut-nav button:hover:not(:disabled) { background: rgba(232, 196, 139, .1); }
  .c-tut-nav button:disabled { opacity: .3; cursor: default; }
  .c-tut-nav button:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--pm-gold); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .c-tut-dots { display: flex; gap: .4rem; }
  .c-tut-dot {
    width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: rgba(205, 194, 171, .28);
  }
  .c-tut-dot.is-on { background: var(--pm-gold); }

  /* THE 480px BREAKPOINT IS GONE, and its going is the point: it re-declared
     the grid and the sprite size, which is a second place the column width was
     decided and a second place to forget. `clamp()` above covers every width
     between 390 and 1280 continuously, so there is one rule. */
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .c-tut-slide { gap: .8rem; }
  }
</style>
